WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Where does the yellow in a fall leaf come from?

2
00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:06.260
Start with a fresh green leaf beside a yellow one.

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.800
A sample coffee-filter strip can help you answer that question.

4
00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:18.807
Your child gets to make a guess, look closely at leaf prints, and sort leaves by

5
00:00:18.807 --> 00:00:24.467
color. You can use the sample strip together to talk about a color you could not see

6
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:25.467
before.

7
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.350
This activity uses no alcohol.

8
00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:34.510
Gather white paper, a spoon, a fresh green leaf, and fallen leaves in different

9
00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.290
colors. If you go outside, go with your child.

10
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.130
Collect where you have permission, away from roads, and take leaves only from plants

11
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.130
you know.

12
00:00:45.483 --> 00:00:51.123
Avoid poison ivy. Dress for the sun and weather, and check for ticks afterward.

13
00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:55.423
Keep small leaf pieces away from children under four.

14
00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:58.183
Call nine one one if choking.

15
00:00:59.523 --> 00:01:02.803
Never taste the leaves, and wash hands with soap afterward.

16
00:01:03.867 --> 00:01:06.487
Show the green and yellow leaves side by side.

17
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:11.207
Ask your child, "Which colors do you think will rub off onto the paper?"

18
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.327
Have them draw or write a guess.

19
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.347
Then fold a sheet of white paper over the green leaf.

20
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:21.883
Have your child rub the folded paper with the back of a spoon.

21
00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:25.363
Keep rubbing until a green print shows through.

22
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:28.963
Repeat with a yellow leaf and a red leaf.

23
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.463
Ask, "Which colors came off onto the paper?"

24
00:01:33.043 --> 00:01:34.303
Compare the prints with their guess.

25
00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:37.567
Put the fallen leaves back on the table.

26
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:41.147
Have your child sort them into piles by color.

27
00:01:42.527 --> 00:01:44.447
Ask them to count the leaves in each pile.

28
00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:49.127
Keep the green leaf and a yellow leaf nearby for the comparison.

29
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:53.323
Look at this drawing of a sample coffee-filter strip.

30
00:01:54.663 --> 00:01:59.583
Fresh green spinach can make a wide green band with a thin yellow one above it.

31
00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:03.543
Ask, "Where do you see yellow on the strip and on the fall leaf?"

32
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:06.867
The yellow was already in the green leaf.

33
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:11.567
Here is a follow-up question for you before your child asks it.

34
00:02:12.647 --> 00:02:16.407
Was a red fall leaf also hiding its red under the green all summer?

35
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.150
Usually, no, and that makes a great follow-up talk.

36
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.810
Yellow pigments sit inside green leaves all summer until green chlorophyll fades in

37
00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:37.650
fall. Most reds are made new in fall from trapped sugars, showing two ways fall

38
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:38.370
colors appear.
